## Terraform state

{% if state_backend.is_local %}
> **State is local.** No backend is configured, so Terraform writes
> `terraform.tfstate` beside these files. That file *is* the mapping between this
> configuration and your real infrastructure — it cannot be shared with a
> teammate, it is not locked against two people applying at once, and if this
> machine is lost the estate becomes unmanageable by Terraform.
>
> Configure a remote backend before you apply anything you intend to keep.
> Re-generate with `--state-backend auto` to have one written for you, or add a
> `backend` block to `versions.tf` by hand and run `terraform init -migrate-state`.
{% elif state_backend.is_complete %}
State is stored remotely via the `{{ state_backend.kind }}` backend configured in
`versions.tf`. Run `terraform init` to initialise it.

Make sure the backing bucket/container has **versioning enabled** — it is the only
thing standing between a bad apply and an unrecoverable estate — and that state
locking is on, so two concurrent applies cannot corrupt it.
{% else %}
The `{{ state_backend.kind }}` backend is declared in `versions.tf` but left
**partially configured** on purpose: this tool does not know your bucket or
account names.

```bash
cp backend.hcl.example backend.hcl   # fill in the blanks
terraform init -backend-config=backend.hcl
```

`terraform init` fails until you do this, which is deliberate — the alternative
is silently falling back to local state.
{% endif %}

## Before you apply

1. **Set your compartment and auth.** Fill in `compartment_id`, `tenancy_ocid`, `user_ocid`,
   `fingerprint`, and `private_key_path` in `terraform.tfvars` (or `TF_VAR_*` env vars). Generate
   an API signing key: `oci setup keys` (or Console → user settings → API keys), then upload the
   public key and note the fingerprint it's issued.
2. **Images resolve automatically** to the latest matching platform image (`data
   "oci_core_images"` in `images.tf`) — nothing to fill in. RHEL/Amazon Linux source VMs map to
   Oracle Linux, the closest binary-compatible OCI platform image.
3. **Set your SSH key.** `admin_ssh_public_key` for Linux instances. Windows instances get an
   auto-generated initial password, retrievable after launch with
   `oci compute instance get-windows-initial-credentials --instance-id <ocid>`.
4. **Review sizing.** Shapes are Flex (E4.Flex general-purpose, E5.Flex memory-optimized for
   database/cache) sized to fit each source VM's vCPU and memory with headroom. Adjust
   `shape_config` in `compute.tf` if needed.
5. **Review networking.** A VCN (`{{ network.vpc_cidr }}`) with public/private subnets, per-tier
   Network Security Groups, and {{ "a NAT gateway for private egress" if network.nat_gateway else "no NAT gateway" }}
   is defined. Instances are placed in the region's first availability domain — see the note at
   the bottom of `networking.tf` for multi-AD placement.

## Deploy

```bash
oci setup keys              # if you don't already have an API signing key
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply
```

